## 2 · Before tuning anything: is 57.0% over the line?

a0-121 published Warden at **127 / 223 = 57.0%** against a 55% ceiling and called
it *"2.0 points short"*. This section is the brief's first instruction, and it is
arithmetic on a number that was already committed — nothing here needed a match
to be run.

### 2.1 · The interval

{{A0121_ROSTER_INTERVALS}}

*Hand-written reading.* **2.0 points on 223 decided matches is 4.3 matches.**
The exact 95% interval on Warden is **50.2% – 63.5%**, and 55% is not near its
edge — it sits a fifth of the way in from the bottom. The exact one-sided test
is the same fact stated as a probability: a contestant whose true rate is exactly
55% wins 127 or more of 223 about **30% of the time**. Three runs in ten.

There is no reading of that number under which it is a finding. A tuning change
made on it is a change that cannot be defended the week after, because the next
run of the same tree would disagree with it three times in ten *with no constant
having moved at all*.

### 2.2 · What ±1 SE was answering, and why it is the wrong bar here

`harness/mirrors` exports `winSE`, every report in this series quotes it, and
a0-121's own table gives Warden **±3.3 points**. That is the correct instrument
for the question those reports asked — *did this number move* — and this report
does not retire it.

It is the wrong bar for a ceiling, twice over:

- **±1 SE is a 68% interval.** It is the bar at which a third of correct trees
  look guilty. A ceiling test that fires on it will nerf something roughly every
  third time it is run against a tree that is exactly on the line.
- **A ceiling is one-sided and fixed.** "57.0 ± 3.3" invites the reader to note
  that 55 is inside one SE and stop there. The question is not whether the two
  numbers are close; it is whether the *true* rate is above 55, and the answer to
  that is a one-sided tail probability — 0.303 — not a distance.

Both columns are printed in §2.1 side by side so the difference is visible rather
than argued.

### 2.3 · Clustering: 32 maps played seven times each, not 223 free matches

An interval computed as though there were 223 independent observations is only
right if there are. The roster contest is 32 seeds × 7 rotations, and a seed is
one map draw — ore field, base placement, wave schedule — held fixed while the
seating rotates. If a map suits Warden, all seven of its matches lean together,
and every interval above is too narrow.

Checked rather than assumed:

{{A0121_CLUSTER}}

*Hand-written reading.* **The seeds do not cluster.** The raw intra-cluster
correlation is slightly *negative*, which is what balanced rotations look like:
seating Warden into all eight chairs across seven lineups washes out the map. The
estimator is floored at zero — "this run is worth more than its match count" is
not a claim an interval should be allowed to make — so the design effect is 1.00
and the plain binomial reading in §2.1 is the fair one. That is a result, not a
formality: had the ICC come back at 0.2 the effective sample would have been 78
matches and the interval half again as wide.

### 2.4 · Where the 2.0 points actually sit

Seven characters occupy eight chairs, so in one rotation of seven each character
holds **two**. Warden's pooled rate mixes six rotations at one chair with one at
two, and a chair is a ticket:

{{A0121_ROTATION}}

{{A0121_SEATCUT}}

*Hand-written reading.* At one chair Warden is **55.5%** — the ceiling, to within
half a point — and the rotation where it holds two is 65.6%. The pooled 57.0%
is, in part, an artifact of a cast that does not divide into eight. This is not
offered as an excuse for Warden and it does not change the verdict, which is
UNRESOLVED on every cut in the table; it is offered because a lane sent to nerf a
character on 2.0 points should know that a third of those points come from a
seating arrangement rather than from the character.

### 2.5 · What the question would cost to answer

{{SAMPLE}}

*Hand-written reading.* This is the shape of the problem and it is unforgiving:
the precision needed goes as the **square** of how close the true rate is to the
line. Separating 65% from 55% is a hundred matches. Separating 57% from 55% is
two and a half thousand. a0-121 did not fail to measure carefully — it measured
at the size every report in this series has used, and that size answers "did it
move" and cannot answer "is it over."

So the honest finding on a0-121's evidence is **"run more matches"**, and §4 runs
them.

### 2.6 · The same test, applied to a0-121's other two verdicts

The interval is not applied only where it is convenient. Re-reading a0-121's
other two target rows the same way:

{{A0121_CLASS_INTERVALS}}

{{A0121_EASY_INTERVALS}}

*Hand-written reading.* Two corrections fall out, one in each direction.

- **The excavator's INSIDE was as thin as Warden's OVER.** 48.6% on 255 matches
