## 3 · a0-121's change was real — and the same instrument says so

A finding that only ever subtracts is a suspicious finding. The seeds a0-121 used
were the same in both its columns, which makes its experiment **paired** — and a
paired experiment read through two independent standard errors throws away its
own design. The matches both trees won, and the ones neither won, carry no
information about the change; only the discordant ones do. McNemar's exact test
is that reading:

{{PAIRED}}

*Hand-written reading.* **a0-121 moved what it said it moved, decisively.** The
hull's −28.9 points on the ship-class contest is p < 0.000001; the Vanguard's
+16.4 is p = 0.000007; Warden's own −16.1 is p = 0.0007. None of that is in
question and none of it is weakened by this report — it is *strengthened*, because
the paired test is more sensitive than the unpaired one a0-121 used on itself.

The finding is narrower than "a0-121 was wrong". It is that **the last two points
were never inside the resolution of the instrument that measured them**, and
a0-121's own report is the thing that shows it: −16.1 at p = 0.0007 and +2.0 at
p = 0.303 came out of the same 223 matches.

